import "core"
import "core"


# A class bound on a declaration variable, reaching a higher-order argument's PARAMETER.
  A declaration's function-typed argument slot holds its INPUT OBJECT, with @w@ annotated by the
  declaration's own variable (@headTo[/A](/fn=/fn[/A=$^A](/w=$/A))@). A call meets that declaration,
  which binds @/A@, and the lambda's pattern @fn(w)@ receives @w@'s type from the call's requirement
  through its slot. Two things stopped that here, and each needs its own row below to see.
  A parameter left at Top is not fatal on its own; a body that never needs the bound runs fine on
  one. It bites when the body BUILDS something whose own declaration requires the bound, since a
  Top argument cannot meet it:
    Failed to build arrow tree from /Data/Maybe/Some(/value=TopType)
      to /Data/Maybe/Some[/T=∀/Data/Algebra/Eq](/value=$/T)
  Both examples for each function below are the same call; only the lambda's body differs, and the
  one that does not need the bound is what says the traversal was never the problem.
# ## Bound through a container's class parameter
  @$A@ is pinned only by @lst@, and it is pinned through a class PARAMETER rather than by being the
  argument's whole type. A parameter that points at a variable is not itself one, so meeting
  @List[$T -> $_A]@ against a @Cons(head= 3, ..)@ bound nothing: the value's own vars are empty
  until the meet fills them. The meet's RESULT does carry @Cons[/T=Integer]@ — materialized through
  the data definition's @head -> $T@ tie — and the spelling says which variable that parameter
  names, so the two are matched up by parameter name.
  Contrast @useEq@ further down, where the same variable is the whole type of an argument: that
  binds directly and always worked.
headTo[$A -> /Data/Algebra/Eq, $B](lst -> /Data/List[$T -> $_A], fn(w -> $_A) -> $_B, dflt -> $_B) -> $_B
headTo(lst= Nil, fn(w), dflt)= dflt
headTo(lst= Cons(head, tail), fn(w), dflt)= fn(w= head)

> headTo(lst= [3], fn(w)= /Data/Maybe/Some(value= w) == /Data/Maybe/Some(value= 3), dflt= False)
  >>> True
> headTo(lst= [], fn(w)= /Data/Maybe/Some(value= w) == /Data/Maybe/Some(value= 3), dflt= False)
  >>> False
> headTo(lst= [3], fn(w)= w + 1, dflt= 0)
  >>> 4
# ## Called from inside a generic definition
  The same call made where the element type is not yet concrete. In @viaGeneric@'s frame @$_A@
  reads as its own bound @∀Eq@, and the parameter was refused because it was not FULLY CONCRETE.
  A bound is resolved knowledge, not a symbolic variable: no value of @$_A@ can fall outside its
  own class, so @∀Eq@ holds at every epoch and is enough to build a @Some@ that meets the @Maybe@
  instance. What stays out is a symbolic variable, which may still resolve to anything.
viaGeneric[$A -> /Data/Algebra/Eq](lst -> /Data/List[$T -> $_A], probe -> $_A) -> Boolean
viaGeneric(lst, probe)= headTo(lst= lst, fn(w)= /Data/Maybe/Some(value= w) == /Data/Maybe/Some(value= probe), dflt= False)

> viaGeneric(lst= [3], probe= 3)
  >>> True
> viaGeneric(lst= [4], probe= 3)
  >>> False
> viaGeneric(lst= ["a"], probe= "a")
  >>> True
# ## The control: the variable IS the argument's type
  Nothing points anywhere, so @$A@ binds directly from @x@. This passed before either fix and is
  the passing half of the pair that pinned the cause on how the variable is bound rather than on
  the class bound itself.
useEq[$A -> /Data/Algebra/Eq, $B](x -> $_A, fn(v -> $_A) -> $_B) -> $_B
useEq(x, fn(v))= fn(v= x)

> useEq(x= 3, fn(v)= /Data/Maybe/Some(value= v) == /Data/Maybe/Some(value= 3))
  >>> True
main{io -> IO}= io.exit(val= 0)
